    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>McAllen is inland, but tropical systems still reach it. Hurricane Dolly (2008) and Hurricane Hanna (2020) both caused damage and flooding in Hidalgo County.</li>
<li>Homeowners policies generally cover wind damage but exclude flood. Flood coverage comes from the NFIP or a private flood policy, and NFIP policies typically have a 30-day waiting period.</li>
<li>Hidalgo County is not in the TWIA catastrophe area, so wind coverage usually comes from your regular insurer.</li>
<li>Check your declarations page now for a wind/hail or named-storm deductible. It is often a percentage of your dwelling limit.</li>
<li>After a storm, document everything before cleanup, prevent further damage, separate wind damage from flood damage, and keep every receipt.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Sitting well inland from the Gulf coast gives McAllen a buffer from storm surge, but it does not make the city immune to tropical weather. Hurricanes and tropical storms that come ashore along the lower Texas coast can push strong winds and heavy rain well inland across the Rio Grande Valley (RGV). This guide covers what McAllen homeowners should know about <strong>hurricane damage</strong>: how coverage is split between wind and flood, which deductibles may apply, how to prepare, and what to do once the storm passes.</p>

<h2>Recent hurricanes that affected McAllen and Hidalgo County</h2>

<p>The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database records several tropical systems in Hidalgo County. Two recent hurricanes stand out.</p>

<h3>Hurricane Dolly, July 23, 2008</h3>

<p>Dolly was the first storm since Bret in 1999 to make landfall along the Deep South Texas barrier islands. According to the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=129365" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>, it left a trail of widespread minor to moderate structural and natural damage across much of the Lower Rio Grande Valley and dumped heavy rainfall that caused flooding, primarily in low-lying and poorly drained locations. That combination of wind damage and rainfall flooding is typical of what a hurricane brings inland, and it is exactly what makes these claims complicated.</p>

<h3>Hurricane Hanna, July 25–26, 2020</h3>

<p>Hanna, the first hurricane of the 2020 Atlantic season, made landfall as a Category 1 storm on the unpopulated Padre Island National Seashore in Kenedy County around 5 p.m. on July 25, with sustained winds of 90 mph near the eyewall. It then moved slowly inland toward the Valley. NCEI records for <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=914181" target="_blank" rel="noopener">northern Hidalgo County</a> and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=914182" target="_blank" rel="noopener">southern Hidalgo County</a> document tropical-storm impacts and major rainfall flooding across the county.</p>

<p>For more local history, visit our <a href="/storm-history/">McAllen storm history page</a> or check events near your address with the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>.</p>

<h2>Wind damage vs. flood damage: the most important distinction</h2>

<p>After a hurricane, the single biggest coverage question is usually whether water damage came from wind or from flood. The answer often decides which policy, if any, pays.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of damage</th><th>Example</th><th>Where coverage usually comes from</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind damage</td><td>Missing shingles, torn metal roofing, broken windows, fallen trees on the house</td><td>Homeowners policy (subject to wind/hail or named-storm deductible)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind-driven rain through a storm-created opening</td><td>Rain enters where wind tore off shingles or broke a window, wetting ceilings and walls</td><td>Often the homeowners policy, depending on policy wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood (surface water)</td><td>Rising water from overflowing streets, drainage ditches, or resacas that enters the home at ground level</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy; generally excluded from homeowners policies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain through an existing defect</td><td>Water entering through an old, deteriorated roof without new storm damage</td><td>Often excluded or limited; depends on policy and facts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sewer or drain backup</td><td>Water backing up through floor drains or toilets during heavy rain</td><td>Often excluded unless the policy has a water backup endorsement</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Many McAllen homes are slab-on-grade, so when surface water rises high enough, it enters the living space directly. Flooding of that kind is generally not covered by a standard homeowners policy. On the other hand, a ceiling that collapses because the roof was opened by wind is a classic wind claim. When both happen in the same storm, careful documentation of where the water entered becomes essential. Our article on <a href="/water-damage-vs-flood-damage-mcallen/">water damage vs. flood damage in McAllen</a> goes deeper on this.</p>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Look for the water line</span><p>Photograph any visible water line on walls, baseboards, furniture, or the exterior. A horizontal line at the same height throughout the room points to rising water. Stains that start at the ceiling and run down point to water coming from above.</p></div>

<h2>Flood insurance basics for McAllen homeowners</h2>

<p>Flood coverage is sold separately, most often through the <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/flood-insurance-basics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Flood Insurance Program</a> (NFIP) or through private flood insurers. A few points to know:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Waiting period.</strong> NFIP policies typically have a 30-day waiting period before coverage begins, with limited exceptions. If you wait until a storm is in the Gulf, it is usually too late for that storm.</li>
<li><strong>You do not need to be in a high-risk zone to buy it.</strong> Flooding from heavy rain can happen outside mapped flood zones, as poorly drained neighborhoods across the Valley have seen.</li>
<li><strong>Separate claims.</strong> A flood claim and a homeowners claim are filed separately, may be handled by different adjusters, and have their own deductibles and rules.</li>
<li><strong>Coverage limits.</strong> NFIP policies have their own limits and categories for building and contents. Read the policy so you know what is and is not included.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Deductibles: wind/hail and named-storm</h2>

<p>Many Texas homeowners policies carry a separate deductible for wind and hail, and some apply a named-storm or hurricane deductible when a named tropical system causes the loss. These deductibles are often a percentage of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit.</p>

<p>For example, a 2% deductible on a home insured for $300,000 is $6,000. A 5% deductible on the same home is $15,000. That is a meaningful amount to absorb after a storm, so look at your declarations page before hurricane season, not after. If you want to estimate where your claim lands, try our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a>.</p>

<p>Policy wording matters here. Some policies define exactly when a named-storm deductible applies (for example, during a set window around a storm warning). Others simply use one wind/hail deductible for all wind events. If the insurer applies a higher deductible than you expected, ask which policy provision it is relying on.</p>

<h2>Why TWIA does not apply in McAllen</h2>

<p>Homeowners on the coast often buy wind coverage through the <a href="https://www.twia.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Windstorm Insurance Association</a> (TWIA). TWIA covers windstorm and hail only in the designated catastrophe area: Aransas, Brazoria, Calhoun, Cameron, Chambers, Galveston, Jefferson, Kenedy, Kleberg, Matagorda, Nueces, Refugio, San Patricio, and Willacy counties, plus part of Harris County. Neighboring Cameron and Willacy counties are in that area. <strong>Hidalgo County is not.</strong></p>

<p>For McAllen, Mission, Edinburg, Pharr, and the rest of Hidalgo County, that typically means:</p>

<ul>
<li>Wind and hail coverage comes from your regular homeowners insurer, or the Texas FAIR Plan where applicable.</li>
<li>TDI windstorm (WPI-8) certification requirements, which apply in the TWIA area, generally do not apply here.</li>
<li>Your hurricane wind claim is handled under your standard policy, with its own deductibles and claim procedures.</li>
</ul>

