<h2>Hurricane preparation checklist for McAllen homes</h2>

<p>The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/safety/hurricane-plan" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service</a> recommends planning well before a storm threatens. From an insurance perspective, preparation also makes a future claim easier to document.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Task</th><th>Why it matters for a claim</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Before the season (by June 1)</td><td>Review your declarations page: deductibles, limits, flood coverage</td><td>Avoid surprises; time to buy flood coverage before the waiting period matters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before the season</td><td>Take date-stamped photos and video of your roof, exterior, and every room</td><td>Shows pre-storm condition if the insurer argues damage was pre-existing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before the season</td><td>Make a home inventory of major contents, with receipts where possible</td><td>Speeds up contents claims and supports values</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before the season</td><td>Trim trees and limbs near the roof; secure loose items</td><td>Reduces damage; insurers may question neglected maintenance</td></tr>
<tr><td>When a storm threatens</td><td>Store policy documents and photos in the cloud or a waterproof bag</td><td>You will need the policy number and contacts quickly</td></tr>
<tr><td>When a storm threatens</td><td>Gather tarps, plywood, plastic sheeting, and a flashlight</td><td>Lets you mitigate damage promptly, which most policies require</td></tr>
<tr><td>When a storm threatens</td><td>Move valuables and electronics off the floor</td><td>Limits flood damage that a homeowners policy may not cover</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What to do after a hurricane passes</h2>

<h3>1. Put safety first</h3>
<p>Stay away from downed power lines and standing water, which may be electrified or contaminated. Do not climb onto a damaged roof. Wait until local officials say it is safe to return if you evacuated.</p>

<h3>2. Document before you clean up</h3>
<p>Photograph and video every area of damage: roof slopes from the ground, broken windows, fences, fallen trees, ceiling stains, wet flooring, and damaged contents. Capture any water lines. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a full room-by-room list.</p>

<h3>3. Prevent further damage</h3>
<p>Tarp roof openings, board broken windows, remove standing water, and start drying the home. Most policies require you to take reasonable steps to protect the property. Keep receipts for supplies, labor, and equipment rentals. Hold on to samples of damaged materials (a piece of carpet, a section of drywall) if you can do so safely.</p>

<h3>4. Report claims to the right insurers</h3>
<p>Report wind damage to your homeowners insurer and flood damage to your flood insurer. If you are not sure which caused what, report to both and let the documentation sort it out. Write down claim numbers, adjuster names, and dates of every conversation.</p>

<h3>5. Track your additional living expenses</h3>
<p>If the home is unlivable due to a covered loss, many policies pay additional living expenses (ALE), such as hotel costs and extra meal expenses above your normal spending. Keep every receipt.</p>

<h3>6. Review the estimate carefully</h3>
<p>Large storms bring adjusters from out of town who may not know local construction or pricing. Check that every damaged area appears in the estimate, quantities make sense, and items like interior drywall, insulation, paint, and flooring are included where water entered. Our guides on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">reviewing a roof insurance estimate</a> and <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> can help.</p>

<h3>7. Know the claim timelines</h3>
<p>Under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, insurers generally must acknowledge a claim and start investigating within 15 days of notice, then accept or reject within 15 business days of receiving requested information (with a possible extension of up to 45 days). After a weather-related catastrophe declared by the Commissioner of Insurance, these deadlines may be extended by 15 days. Expect slower response times after a major hurricane, and follow up in writing.</p>

<h2>Common hurricane claim disputes</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Wind vs. flood causation.</strong> The insurer attributes interior water to flood (excluded) rather than wind-driven rain through a storm-created opening.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing damage.</strong> The insurer says roof damage existed before the storm. Pre-storm photos help.</li>
<li><strong>Deductible application.</strong> A higher named-storm deductible applied when the policy language may not support it.</li>
<li><strong>Scope gaps.</strong> Estimates that miss interior damage, fences, outbuildings, or contents.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Heavy depreciation on roofs and contents, reducing the initial payment.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you disagree with the amount, you may be able to request a supplement or invoke appraisal under your policy. If the claim is denied, read the letter for the specific reason and policy provision cited. For legal questions such as bad faith or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help after a hurricane</h2>

<p>A licensed Texas public adjuster represents the policyholder only. After a hurricane, that can mean documenting wind and water damage throughout the property, separating wind-caused damage from flood damage in the record, preparing an itemized estimate, and handling communication with the insurer while you focus on your family and home. See our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> service page for details.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Outcomes always depend on the policy and the facts, and no one can promise a particular result.</p>

